WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.667
Convolutional neural networks.

2
00:00:02.967 --> 00:00:05.233
The idea that made deep learning famous.

3
00:00:05.233 --> 00:00:06.367
Let's see the trick.

4
00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:13.933
A dense layer on a two hundred twenty four pixel image needs about a hundred fifty thousand weights per neuron.

5
00:00:14.233 --> 00:00:18.903
It also has to relearn the same bright edge separately at every position.

6
00:00:18.903 --> 00:00:21.333
A convolution needs just nine weights.

7
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:27.467
A convolutional layer learns one small filter, and slides it across the whole input.

8
00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:34.033
Each stop produces one number, and together they form a feature map that lights up wherever the pattern occurs.

9
00:00:34.333 --> 00:00:35.672
Same weights everywhere.

10
00:00:35.672 --> 00:00:36.900
That's weight sharing.

11
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.500
Here's a tiny example.

12
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.000
A five by five input with a bright region on the right.

13
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.267
The three by three filter sits on the top-left patch.

14
00:00:46.567 --> 00:00:50.400
It multiplies and sums, and writes one value into the feature map.

15
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:53.667
Slide on, and the map is large where the edge sits.

16
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:56.567
Now stack many of these layers.

17
00:00:56.867 --> 00:00:59.033
Early layers learn edges and blobs.

18
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:02.300
Middle layers combine them into textures and parts.

19
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.541
Deep layers see whole objects.

20
00:01:04.541 --> 00:01:10.300
Pooling between stages shrinks the data, so deeper filters cover a wider receptive field.

21
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.949
The grid needn't be two dimensional.

22
00:01:13.949 --> 00:01:16.533
Here, a Conv1d reads raw light curves.

23
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:21.000
The first layer learns sixteen filters that slide along the flux readings.

24
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:25.267
Pooling and a second layer shrink the length while seeing wider stretches.

25
00:01:25.567 --> 00:01:29.200
A final linear layer decides: transit, or no transit.

26
00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.000
Here's a real comparison.

27
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:36.433
A baseline with two hundred hand-built features reached eighty nine percent.

28
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:40.733
The Conv1d model reads the raw curve and reaches ninety five.

29
00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:46.700
A transit dip looks the same wherever it occurs, which is exactly what convolution assumes.

30
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.100
And its first-layer filters learned dip detectors without being told.

31
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.100
Let's recap.

32
00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.700
A small filter slides across the input.

33
00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.100
Weight sharing finds a pattern anywhere.

34
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.767
Layers build from edges up to objects.

35
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:04.112
And Conv1d does the same for time series.

36
00:02:04.112 --> 00:02:05.300
Next up: ResNet.
